# 4. Standard Module Structure

Every domain module must follow a consistent internal structure.

Example:

```text
modules/
└── transactions/
    ├── domain/
    │   ├── entities/
    │   │   └── transaction.ts
    │   ├── value-objects/
    │   │   ├── transaction-type.ts
    │   │   └── transaction-status.ts
    │   ├── repositories/
    │   │   └── transaction-repository.ts
    │   ├── services/
    │   │   └── transaction-domain-service.ts
    │   ├── events/
    │   │   └── transaction-events.ts
    │   └── errors/
    │       └── transaction-errors.ts
    │
    ├── application/
    │   ├── commands/
    │   │   ├── create-expense.ts
    │   │   ├── create-income.ts
    │   │   ├── update-transaction.ts
    │   │   └── delete-transaction.ts
    │   ├── queries/
    │   │   ├── get-transaction.ts
    │   │   └── list-transactions.ts
    │   ├── dto/
    │   └── mappers/
    │
    ├── infrastructure/
    │   ├── persistence/
    │   │   ├── transaction-repository.impl.ts
    │   │   └── transaction-mapper.ts
    │   └── api/
    │
    ├── presentation/
    │   ├── screens/
    │   ├── components/
    │   ├── hooks/
    │   └── view-models/
    │
    └── index.ts
```

### Module Rules

* `domain/` must be framework-independent.
* `application/` coordinates use cases.
* `infrastructure/` implements external dependencies.
* `presentation/` consumes application services.
* `index.ts` exposes only the module's public API.
* Internal files must not be imported directly by unrelated modules.
* Do not create a universal `services/` folder containing unrelated business logic.
* Do not create a universal `utils/` folder for domain logic.
* Do not create a universal `models/` folder containing every entity in the application.

